-- Passage rg10-character-setting-plot-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('9519b9c5-6bf6-5851-a29a-86e757ab16fe', 'rg10-character-setting-plot-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-character-setting-plot'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Second Chair$q$, $q$For two years I have sat one seat to the left of Imani,
close enough to hear her breathe before each entrance,
close enough to watch her bow arm and copy it.
I tell myself I do not want her chair.
I tell myself a lot of things.

In October the conductor gave the solo to her again.
I clapped the way you clap for weather,
politely, because it was going to happen anyway.
That night I practiced the passage until my fingertips burned,
not to play it better than she did,
but to prove that I could have.

In March, Imani's string snapped during the concert.
It cracked like a twig in the quiet hall,
and she turned to me with her whole face asking.
I handed her my cello without thinking.
Then I sat with her broken one across my knees
and listened to my own instrument sing in someone else's hands.

It sounded good. It sounded better than I make it sound.
Afterward she thanked me twice, then a third time,
and asked whether I would show her how I shape the low notes,
because she has been trying to copy me for two years.

I have not decided yet what to do with that.
I keep thinking about the chair to my right,
and how it might not be a ladder after all.$q$, 'draft')
