insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('5d68c5d6-fb15-5827-aaaa-255b58b751af', 'rg10-character-setting-plot-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-character-setting-plot'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Last Walk$q$, $q$The moving truck was coming at eight on Saturday, and on Friday night Kwame Asante was the only person in the apartment who seemed to be packing. His parents were at work until ten. That left Kwame, seventeen, in charge of the kitchen, the hall closet, and his sister Ama, who was nine and had been assigned exactly one box.

Kwame was good at being in charge. He liked how his mother said "I don't know what we'd do without you," and he liked, a little guiltily, how it made him feel older than his friends. He did not like the move. The new apartment was across the city, forty minutes by bus from the courts where he had played every summer night since sixth grade. He had not said this to anyone. Saying it would not make the rent any lower.

At nine he checked on Ama. Her box was empty. Everything she had packed that afternoon was back on her shelves, lined up neatly, as if nothing were happening.

"Ama," he said, sharper than he meant, "the truck comes in eleven hours."

She did not turn around. "I'm not done with it here yet."

He opened his mouth to argue and found he had nothing to say, because it was exactly the sentence he had been holding in his own chest all week. He sat down on her floor instead.

"Okay," he said after a while. "What aren't you done with?"

She listed things: the corner store cat, the crack in the sidewalk shaped like a fish, the window where you could see the trains. Kwame got his phone. Together they walked the block in the warm dark and photographed every item on her list, and then, without explaining, he added the basketball courts.

Back home, Ama packed her box in ten minutes. Kwame sat on the floor a little longer, scrolling through the pictures.$q$, 'draft')
